Peripheral Arterial Disease (PAD)
Cardiovascular
Reproducible exertional calf pain, Absent distal pulses, Dependent rubor/Elevation pallor, ABI <0.90

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CDV-033
Reason for Visit: Exquisite pain in the toes while resting in bed, causing severe sleep deprivation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified an elderly patient with severe, unrelenting rest pain in the feet, raising the immediate suspicion of critical limb ischemia. Urgent in-person physical assessment was strictly required to evaluate limb viability, rule out acute embolic occlusion, and manage severe ischemic pain that could not be safely deferred until the next available in-hours appointment, as irreversible tissue loss could occur.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My toes feel like they are on fire when I lie down, and the only way to get a minute of peace is to hang my leg out of the bed."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a long-standing history of intermittent claudication characterized by reproducible calf pain on exertion, which has progressively worsened over the last year. For the past 48 hours, the patient has developed severe ischemic rest pain in the right foot, particularly the toes, whenever lying flat in bed. The pain is exquisitely sharp and burning, partially relieved by hanging the leg over the side of the bed (gravity dependency). KEY SYMPTOMS: Intermittent claudication characterized by reproducible calf pain on exertion. Prompt relief of claudication pain with rest (historically). Severe resting pain in the distal extremity.
Functional Status: Severely compromised due to sleep deprivation and pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; immobility and sleep deprivation impairing ability to perform basic self-care.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/85 | HR: 80 | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 98 percent | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Fatigued, sitting on the edge of the bed with the right leg hanging down, rubbing the foot.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular/Extremities: Diminished popliteal pulse and completely absent distal peripheral pulses (dorsalis pedis, posterior tibial) on the right. The skin on the right lower extremity is cool, pale, and shiny, with complete loss of hair over the toes and shins. Delayed capillary refill time in the right toes (>4 seconds). Buerger's test is positive: striking pallor on limb elevation to 45 degrees, followed by dependent rubor when the leg is lowered. Early atrophic skin changes noted on the right great toe, without frank ulceration. DIAGNOSTIC TETRAD: Reproducible exertional calf pain (by history), Absent distal pulses, Dependent rubor/Elevation pallor, ABI <0.90 (anticipated <0.4 based on critical rest pain).
Environmental Observations: Multiple blankets on the bed, but patient avoids covering the right foot due to hypersensitivity.
Medication Reconciliation: Current smoker (20/day). Not on antiplatelet therapy.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Peripheral Arterial Disease - Status: Exacerbated (Critical Limb Ischemia)
Differentials:

Acute Arterial Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of a long-standing claudication history and the classic positional relief of the rest pain, rather than a sudden, acute "thunderclap" onset of a cold, pulseless, paralyzed limb.

Diabetic Peripheral Neuropathy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The profound vascular signs (absent pulses, dependent rubor, elevation pallor, delayed capillary refill) which point to macroscopic flow limitation rather than isolated microscopic nerve damage.

Deep Vein Thrombosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The limb is cool, pale, and pulseless, rather than swollen, warm, and erythematous with a palpable venous cord.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the hallmark signs of critical limb ischemia secondary to end-stage peripheral arterial disease: ischemic rest pain relieved by dependency, absent distal pulses, elevation pallor, and dependent rubor. The severe, unrelenting nature of the pain and the imminent threat of gangrene necessitated an urgent after-hours physical assessment. Evaluating the limb's current viability was critical to safely prescribe strong analgesia for the night and to generate an urgent, fast-tracked referral to Vascular Surgery, precluding any unsafe delay until routine hours.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed a short course of opioid analgesia (e.g., Oxycodone) to manage the severe ischemic rest pain overnight. Initiated Aspirin 100mg stat. Generated an urgent referral to Vascular Surgery for an Ankle-Brachial Index (ABI) assessment and potential revascularization.
Positive Behavioral Change: Instructed the patient to completely cease smoking immediately. By substituting the physical habit of smoking with drinking a glass of water every time a craving hits, the patient stops the acute chemical vasospasm that is further choking off the blood supply to their dying toes. The reward is a tangible chance at saving the limb from amputation.
Home-Based Services: None.
DME/Safety: Advised patient to sleep in a recliner chair tonight to utilize gravity for perfusion.
Follow-up: Urgent Vascular Surgery outpatient review. GP review in 24 hours to monitor pain control.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the foot turns completely white, blue, or black, or if the foot becomes completely numb or paralyzed, the patient must attend the nearest Emergency Department immediately.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis relies on profound clinical signs; formal vascular imaging and ABI measurements are required to plan surgical intervention; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Critical limb ischemia is a limb-threatening emergency. Without aggressive medical and surgical intervention, and absolute smoking cessation, amputation is highly likely.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Sleep in a chair with your leg hanging down. Do not expose the foot to extreme cold or direct heat. Seek emergency care immediately if the toes turn black or the foot goes numb.

